<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-trig-identities" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

    <title>Trigonometric Identities</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this section, we will learn to verify the fundamental trigonometric identities and to
            simplify trigonometric expressions using algebra and the identities.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection>
        <title>Textbook Reference</title>
        <p> 
            This relates to content in <section-mark/>9.1 of 
            <url href="https://openstax.org/details/books/algebra-and-trigonometry-2e" visual="http://tiny.cc/111Z-Textbook">Algebra and Trigonometry 2e</url>.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <exercises xml:id="prep-trig-identities">
		<title>Preparation Exercises</title>
        <introduction>
            Answer the following. These questions are intended to check 
            the prerequisite skills needed to complete the rest of the lab.
        </introduction>
		<exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                Sketch a graph of <m>f(x) = 4\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{3}\left(x-2\right)\right)+x</m>.
                </p>
                <image width="60%">
                    <description>
                        <p>
                            A blank graph.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                    <latex-image>
                        <![CDATA[
                            \begin{tikzpicture}
                            \begin{axis}[
                            framed,
                            xmin=-8,xmax=8,
                            ymin=-10,ymax=8,
                            xtick={-6,-4,...,6},
                            minor xtick={-7,-6,...,7},
                            ytick={-8,-6,...,6},
                            minor ytick={-9,-8,...,7},
                            grid=both,
                            ]
                            \end{axis}
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                            ]]>
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
		<exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>g(x) = \frac{x-4}{x-1}</m>, evaluate <m>g\left(\frac{x-2}{x-3}\right)</m>
                    and simplify to lowest form.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
		<exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which of the six fundamental trigonometric functions are odd functions?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
		<exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    True or false: "Since we know that <m>\sin^2(x) + \cos^2(x) = 1</m>, then if we
                    take the square root on both sides, it must be true that <m>\sin(x)+\cos(x)=1</m>."
                </p>
                <p>
                    Explain your reasoning.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
		<exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Factor the expression <m>1-S^2</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
	</exercises>

	<exercises xml:id="practice-trig-identities">
		<title>Practice Exercises</title>
		<exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <statement>
                <sidebyside widths="60% 35%">
                    <p>
                        Use technology to verify the identity <m>sec(x)−cos(x) = sin(x)tan(x)</m> 
                        by following the steps below.
                        <ul>
                            <li>Make a graph of the left-hand side of the equation, <m>y = \sec(x) - \cos(x)</m>, using technology.</li>
                            <li>Make a graph of the right-hand side of the equation, <m>y = \sin(x)\tan(x)</m>, using technology.</li>
                            <li>Describe what you see on your graph. If the two graphs above overlap, then that verifies that both sides of the equation are identical.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <image width="60%">
                        <description>
                            <p>
                                A blank graph.
                            </p>
                        </description>
                        <latex-image>
                            <![CDATA[
                                \begin{tikzpicture}
                                \begin{axis}[
                                framed,
                                xmin=-9.424778,xmax=9.424778,
                                ymin=-10,ymax=10,
                                xtick={-6.2831853,-3.1415927,0,3.1415927,6.2831853},
                                xticklabels={$-2\pi$,$-\pi$,$0$,$\pi$,$2\pi$},
                                ytick={-8,-6,...,8},
                                grid=both,
                                ]
                                \end{axis}
                                \end{tikzpicture}
                                ]]>
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </sidebyside>
            </statement>
		</exercise>
		<exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Show that <m>\sin(x)-\cos(x)=\left(\tan(x)-1\right)\sec^2(x)</m> is <em>not</em>
                    an identity by creating a graph in Desmos. Discuss with your neighbor what the 
                    graph shows that makes it not an identity.
                </p>
            </statement>
		</exercise>
		<exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Verify the trigonometric identities.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\tan(\theta)\csc(\theta)=\sec(\theta)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\sec(-\beta) + \tan(-\beta) = (1-\sin(\beta))\sec(\beta)</m>jj
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\frac{\sec(\alpha)}{\csc(\alpha)} = \tan(\alpha)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\frac{\tan^2(\gamma)}{\tan^2(\gamma)+1} = \sin^2(\gamma)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\tan(\phi)\sin(\phi)+\cos(\phi)=\sec(\phi)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>\frac{\csc(\mu)\cos(\mu)}{\tan(\mu)+\cot(\mu)} = \cos^2(\mu)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
		</exercise>
        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Factor the trigonometric expressions.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>4-9\sin^2(x)</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        <m>2\sin^2(x)-\sin(x)-1</m>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    
    <subsection xml:id="definitions-trig-identities">
        <title>Definitions</title>
        <definition>
            <title>Identity</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    An <term>identity</term> is an equation that is always true, regardless of the
                    value inputted.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
        <definition>
            <title>Identities for tangent, cotangent, secant, and cosecant</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Recall that the functions <term>tangent</term>, <term>cotangent</term>, 
                    <term>secant</term>, and <term>cosecant</term> can all be written in terms of 
                    sine and/or cosine.
                    <ul>
                        <li><m>\tan(x) = \frac{\sin(x)}{\cos(x)}</m></li>
                        <li><m>\sec(x) = \frac{1}{\cos(x)}</m></li>
                        <li><m>\cot(x) = \frac{\cos(x)}{\sin(x)}</m></li>
                        <li><m>\csc(x) = \frac{1}{\sin(x)}</m></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
        <definition>
            <title>Odd and even trigonometric functions</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Recall that sine, tangent, cotangent, and cosecant are all <term>odd functions</term>.
                    <ul>
                        <li><m>\sin(x) = -\sin(x)</m></li>
                        <li><m>\tan(x) = -\tan(x)</m></li>
                        <li><m>\cot(x) = -\cot(x)</m></li>
                        <li><m>\csc(x) = -\csc(x)</m></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Cosine and secant are <term>even functions</term>
                    <ul>
                        <li><m>\cos(x) = -\cos(x)</m></li>
                        <li><m>\sec(x) = -\sec(x)</m></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
        <definition>
            <title>Pythagorean identities</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Recall the three <term>Pythagorean Identities</term>:
                    <ul>
                        <li><m>\sin^2(\theta) + \cos^2(\theta) = 1</m></li>
                        <li><m>\tan^2(\theta) + 1 = \sec^2(\theta)</m></li>
                        <li><m>\cot^2(\theta) + 1 = \csc^2(\theta)</m></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Note that the first relationship is often written as either 
                    <m>\sin^2(\theta) = 1 - \cos^2(\theta)</m> or <m>\cos^2(\theta) = 1 - \sin^2(\theta)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
	</subsection>
        
    <exercises xml:id="exit-trig-identities">
        <title>Exit Exercises</title>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What makes an equation "an identity"?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How would you decide if the equation 
                    <md>\sin(\theta)+\cos(\theta) = \frac{1}{\csc(\theta)+\sec(\theta)}</md>
                    is an identity or not?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Verify the identity  
                    <md>-\frac{\cos(-\kappa)}{\sec(\kappa)} = \sin^2(\kappa) - 1</md>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Verify the identity  
                    <md>-\frac{\cos(x)}{1 - \sin(x)} = \frac{1+\sin(x)}{\cos(x)}</md>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <reading-questions>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>On a scale of 1-5, how are you feeling with the concepts related to the graphical behaviors of functions?</p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </reading-questions>
</section>
